            id: 'w8_d1_q1',
            title: 'Catch async errors that escape error boundaries',
            problemStatement: 'Error boundaries don\'t catch errors in async code (event handlers, setTimeout, Promise rejections). List the four types of errors that escape error boundaries with examples. Then show two patterns to bring async errors back into the error boundary: (1) storing the error in state and rethrowing in render, (2) using useErrorBoundary from react-error-boundary library.',
            inputOutputExample: '// Escapes boundary:\nuseEffect(() => {\n  fetchData().catch(err => {\n    // This error is NOT caught by ErrorBoundary\n    console.error(err);\n  });\n}, []);\n\n// Fix 1: store and rethrow\nconst [error, setError] = useState(null);\nif (error) throw error; // re-enters render path → caught by boundary\n\n// Fix 2: react-error-boundary\nconst { showBoundary } = useErrorBoundary();\nfetchData().catch(showBoundary);',
            hint: 'Error boundaries catch: errors during render, in lifecycle methods, in constructors of children. They do NOT catch: event handlers, async code, errors in the boundary itself, or server-side rendering errors. The useState+throw pattern is the standard workaround.',
            whyAsked: 'The gap between "error boundaries catch render errors" and understanding what they DON\'T catch is where most React developers have blind spots. Interviewers specifically probe this.',
