            title: 'Implement double-submit cookie CSRF protection',
            problemStatement: 'Implement CSRF protection for a Next.js API using the double-submit cookie pattern: (1) On GET requests to sensitive pages, generate a cryptographically random token and set it as a non-HttpOnly cookie (so JS can read it). (2) JavaScript reads the token and sends it as a custom header (X-CSRF-Token) on every POST/PUT/DELETE. (3) The server validates that the header value matches the cookie value.',
            inputOutputExample: '// Server: set cookie on page load\nconst csrfToken = crypto.randomUUID();\nres.setHeader(\'Set-Cookie\', `csrf=${csrfToken}; SameSite=Strict; Secure`);\n\n// Client: read and send with every mutation\nconst token = document.cookie.match(/csrf=([^;]+)/)?.[1];\nfetch(\'/api/transfer\', {\n  method: \'POST\',\n  headers: { \'X-CSRF-Token\': token },\n});\n\n// Server: validate\nif (req.headers[\'x-csrf-token\'] !== parseCookie(req)[\'csrf\']) return 403;',
            hint: 'The double-submit pattern works because a cross-origin attacker cannot read cookies (same-origin policy). They can forge the request but cannot include the correct X-CSRF-Token header value since they can\'t read the cookie. SameSite=Strict provides additional protection.',
            whyAsked: 'CSRF is a top-10 web vulnerability. The double-submit cookie pattern is one of two standard mitigations (the other being synchronizer tokens). Interviewers who care about security ask this to see if you understand the attack model, not just the header name.',
