            problemStatement: 'You have a React component that runs a 500ms image processing algorithm (simulate with a heavy loop) synchronously, blocking the UI. Move the computation to a dedicated Web Worker. Show: creating the worker, sending data via postMessage, receiving the result, and cleaning up the worker. Use TypeScript types for the message protocol.',
            inputOutputExample: '// worker.ts:\nself.onmessage = (e: MessageEvent<{ pixels: Uint8ClampedArray }>) => {\n  const result = processImage(e.data.pixels); // 500ms, runs off-main thread\n  self.postMessage({ result });\n};\n\n// Component:\nconst worker = new Worker(new URL(\'./worker.ts\', import.meta.url));\nworker.postMessage({ pixels: imageData.data });\nworker.onmessage = (e) => setProcessed(e.data.result);\nreturn () => worker.terminate(); // cleanup',
            hint: 'Workers communicate only via postMessage — no shared memory by default. Data is structured-cloned (copied) unless you use Transferable objects (ArrayBuffer). import.meta.url syntax for worker creation works with Vite. Always terminate workers in cleanup.',
            whyAsked: 'Web Workers are the solution for CPU-intensive frontend work. The postMessage protocol, Transferable objects, and cleanup are all interview-probe points that distinguish theory from practice.',
