            title: 'Transfer an ArrayBuffer (zero-copy) to a Worker',
            problemStatement: 'The previous question copies the ArrayBuffer on each postMessage (structured clone). For a 50MB image buffer, this copy takes 100ms and wastes memory. Refactor to use Transferable objects: transfer ownership of the ArrayBuffer to the worker, then transfer results back. Show the before (copy) and after (transfer) postMessage calls and explain what "transferred" means for the original reference.',
            inputOutputExample: '// Before (copy — 100ms overhead for 50MB):\nworker.postMessage({ pixels: buffer });\n// buffer still usable in main thread\n\n// After (transfer — ~0ms, zero-copy):\nworker.postMessage({ pixels: buffer }, [buffer]); // buffer is in transfer list\n// buffer.byteLength === 0 now — ownership transferred, main thread can\'t use it\n\n// Worker sends result back via transfer:\nself.postMessage({ result: resultBuffer }, [resultBuffer]);',
            hint: 'The second argument to postMessage is a transfer list. ArrayBuffers in the list are transferred (not copied) — the sender\'s reference becomes detached (byteLength = 0). This is essential for performance with large binary data (images, audio, video frames).',
            whyAsked: 'Transferable objects are an advanced Web Worker topic that matters for real performance-sensitive work (image/video processing, WASM). Most candidates don\'t know about them — knowing them signals real-world Worker usage.',
