            problemStatement: 'Write a Service Worker fetch handler for API GET requests that implements the stale-while-revalidate caching strategy: respond immediately from cache (if available), simultaneously fetch a fresh version from the network and update the cache. For cache misses, wait for the network and cache the result. For network failures, fall back to cache.',
            inputOutputExample: 'self.addEventListener(\'fetch\', (event) => {\n  if (!event.request.url.includes(\'/api/\')) return; // only intercept API\n  event.respondWith(\n    caches.open(\'api-v1\').then(async (cache) => {\n      const cached = await cache.match(event.request);\n      const networkFetch = fetch(event.request).then(res => {\n        cache.put(event.request, res.clone());\n        return res;\n      }).catch(() => cached); // fallback on network failure\n      return cached || networkFetch; // respond immediately from cache or wait\n    })\n  );\n});',
            hint: 'SWR: return the cached response immediately if it exists, and fire the network request in parallel. Don\'t await the network fetch before responding. res.clone() is needed because the Response body can only be consumed once.',
            whyAsked: 'Stale-while-revalidate is the most useful Service Worker pattern for apps that need both speed and freshness. Interviewers probe the res.clone() detail and the parallel fetch pattern as litmus tests.',
