            inputOutputExample: '// Online fetch: store in IndexedDB\nconst notes = await fetchNotes();\nawait db.notes.putAll(notes);\n\n// Offline write: queue instead of sending\nif (!navigator.onLine) {\n  await db.syncQueue.add({ type: \'UPDATE\', note, timestamp: Date.now() });\n}\n\n// On reconnect:\nwindow.addEventListener(\'online\', async () => {\n  const queue = await db.syncQueue.getAll();\n  for (const op of queue.sort((a, b) => a.timestamp - b.timestamp)) {\n    await syncToServer(op);\n  }\n  await db.syncQueue.clear();\n});',
            hint: 'The sync queue stores operations with timestamps for ordering. Last-write-wins conflict resolution: the operation with the highest timestamp wins. navigator.onLine and the online event are used to detect connectivity. Process the queue sequentially to preserve order.',
            whyAsked: 'Offline-first architecture is the definitive test of whether you understand IndexedDB, Service Workers, and sync patterns together. It\'s asked in interviews for apps that target unreliable network environments.',
